From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 18:03:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 017/190] segacd: chime at power-on, because the bell has no
 other trigger

EmuTOS rings the bell only when a BEL character reaches the VT52
driver, and nothing in GEM, EmuDesk or EmuCON ever writes one. Real TOS
alerts are silent too, so making them beep would be a deviation rather
than a fix. That left the bell unreachable and therefore untestable.

A single chime from segacd_sound_init() fixes that: it exercises the
whole audio path -- chip enable, wave RAM, channel programming -- at
the earliest possible moment, so one listen at boot answers whether
this machine can make a sound.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index dfdf8d4..c54ddfd 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -142,6 +142,12 @@ void segacd_sound_init(void)
     PCM_ST  = 0x00;                     /* sample begins at wave RAM 0 */
     PCM_ONOFF = 0xFF;                   /* every channel off for now */
     pcm_countdown = 0;
+
+    /* Power-on chime. Not decoration: the bell's only other trigger is
+     * a BEL character reaching the VT52 driver, and nothing in GEM, the
+     * desktop or EmuCON ever writes one -- so without this there is no
+     * way to find out whether this machine can make a sound at all. */
+    segacd_bell();
 }
 
 static void pcm_beep(UBYTE frames, UBYTE volume)
